<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Begada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1445">1445</a>–<a href="/years/1511">1511</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Mahmud Shah I of Gujarat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud Begada was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> from 1458 to 1511, the greatest ruler of the Muzaffarid dynasty. He conquered Junagadh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champaner">Champaner</a>, which he made a new capital. In alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> fleet he fought the Portuguese, winning at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaul">Chaul</a> in 1508 but losing at Diu in 1509, a defeat that opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> to Portuguese naval power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Fateh Khan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a> on 20 December 1445, he was the son of Muhammad Shah II and Bibi Mughali, a daughter of Jam Tughlaq of the Samma dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thatta">Thatta</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>. When the nobles deposed his uncle Daud Khan after a reign of only days, they placed Fateh Khan on the throne at little more than thirteen as Mahmud Shah I. His surname Begada is explained either by his moustaches, twisted like a bullock's horns, or by the Gujarati words for two forts, after his capture of Junagadh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavagadh">Pavagadh</a>. He styled himself sultan of the land and sultan of the sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1462 he twice marched to help Nizam Shah Bahmani against Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-khalji">Mahmud Khalji</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, who withdrew each time. In 1464 he took the pirate hill fort of Barur near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daman">Daman</a> and left its chief in place under tribute. At Junagadh he forced the Chudasama ruler Ra Mandalika III to submit in 1467, and after a siege of nearly two years the Ra surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girnar">Girnar</a> in 1472-73 and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>; Sorath became a crown possession. In 1473 he took Dwarka and Bet Dwarka, destroying temples, and Raja Bhim was later captured and killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a>.</p><p>In 1487 his army sacked Kuva, the Jhala capital, after the death of Vaghoji Jhala, an event remembered as Kuva-no-Kehr. Against the Portuguese he allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In 1508 his squadron under Malik Ayyaz and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> fleet won the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaul">Chaul</a>, but in 1509 the Portuguese won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-diu-1509">Battle of Diu</a>, an event that marks the start of European colonialism in Asia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mahmud doubled the army, ousted landholders only for proved oppression, forbade soldiers to borrow at interest, kept the roads safe and opened hospitals. He founded Mustafabad at Junagadh, Muhammadabad at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champaner">Champaner</a> and Mehmudabad, and his court's nobles built suburbs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a> and the fortress of Diu. A staunch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a>, he died on 23 November 1511 before a Shia embassy from Shah Ismail Safavi entered the city. Later writers gave him the highest place among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> kings, and Udayaraja's Sanskrit Rājavinoda presented him as an Indic king. European travellers spread exaggerated tales of his appetite and of his being fed poison from childhood.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahmud Begada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Mahmud Begada</b>'s sobriquet Begada literally means the conqueror of two forts, probably after he took Girnar (Junagadh) and Champaner (Pavagadh).</blockquote><a href="/empire/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Mahmud Begada</b> of the Gujarat Sultanate took Champaner and Pavagadh in 1484, the defeated Chauhan Rajput rulers' descendants withdrew into the hills to the east.</blockquote><a href="/state/baria-state">Baria State</a></li><li><blockquote>When Sultan <b>Mahmud Begada</b> of Gujarat took Champaner in 1484, members of the Khichi Chauhan ruling family fled east and founded the states of Chhota Udaipur and Baria.</blockquote><a href="/state/chhota-udaipur-state">Chhota Udaipur State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.02,72.58&amp;year=1487" aria-label="Ahmedabad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6489 1314 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mahmud Begada"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6939 1584h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6939 1584h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.02,72.58&amp;year=1487">What was here in 1487? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
